  "core_message": "A railroad foreman survived an iron rod through his skull, yet modern evidence suggests he slowly rebuilt his personality over twelve years.",
  "mood": "curious",
  "title": "19‑lb Iron Rod, 12 Years Alive: The Gage Paradox",
  "description": "In 1848, Phineas Gage was blasting rock for a railroad when a nineteen pound iron rod tore through his brain. He lived for another twelve years, but his friends said he was no longer himself. \n\nNew research challenges the old story of permanent madness, suggesting Gage actually recovered more than we once thought. This video traces the accident, the controversy, and the surprising truth about the most famous brain injury in history.\n\nBased on the Wikipedia article \"Phineas Gage\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phineas_Gage), licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.",
